WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.550
And in entrepreneurship, there's never a

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.000
clear answer, it seems like.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.680
>> [snorts]

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.840
>> Decision-making is really hard because

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.960
we are we are not

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.360
ever exposed to it

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.600
growing up.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.960
Um our parents put us in bubbles, they

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.680
protect us from all pain.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.080
They make decisions for us. I went to

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.240
college.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.640
Look, I got a I got a 23 on my ACT. I

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.760
would never get in Cornell today even

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.360
with my track ability. I got into an Ivy

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.759
League school because I could run track,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.560
period.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:37.040
When I got there, I met a lot of

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.800
geniuses, kids who got

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:44.680
34s, 36s, perfect scores on their ACTs,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.680
same with SATs, brilliant kids.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.720
They did not know how to figure out what

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.320
bus to take

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.600
to class.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.840
They didn't know what they should get

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.680
for dinner

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.920
at the buffet.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.200
They didn't know how to get on and

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.360
schedule and research their classes

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.519
because their parents did everything for

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.680
them. They put them in a total bubble

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.400
and the parents' mission was to get

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.480
their kid in Cornell.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.120
So the kid was a robot that took orders

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.400
from their parents and when they got to

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.320
Cornell and they learned about

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.120
weed and sex, well actually none of

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.240
these nerds were interested in that, but

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.960
video games, like they got totally

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.320
obsessed with it and they didn't know

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.160
how to interact with it. They didn't

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.120
know how to make a decision not to do it

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.320
and stay up all night. And so yeah, it

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.680
was

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.160
pretty crazy to watch how so many people

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.680
are totally sheltered from making actual

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.280
decisions and learning and living with

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.840
the consequences of it. And

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.800
decision-making is a is a learned

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.720
ability. You start the best way to start

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.000
making decisions is when the

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.000
consequences are very low

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:53.160
and not when they're high.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:55.280
Um because you get you get used to

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.560
negative feedback if you make a bad

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.720
decision and positive feedback if you

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.400
make a good one.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.000
So, yeah, um

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.080
it's it's absolutely reps. It's

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.640
absolutely reps.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.840
Same with delegation, hiring. Like,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.840
we're never

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.280
there's never a world where we as

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.959
high schoolers

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.560
walk into a classroom and they say, "Oh,

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:14.920
yeah, you guys have a seven-page paper

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.240
to write tomorrow. Why don't you find a

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.480
freshman to write that paper for you?"

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.160
Or you show up to track practice and

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.560
they're like, "Oh, yeah, we got a

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.520
circuit and four 400s today.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.520
Why don't you and then we were going to

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.640
do weights, but why don't you find

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.120
somebody else to lift weights after

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.320
practice for you?"

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.880
There's there's no absolutely zero

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.680
practice delegating tasks to other

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.880
people in the real world.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.120
So, 99% of people do not know how to do

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.200
it.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.720
So, if you can lead other people and

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.959
tell them what to do and make them want

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.720
to do it and excited to do it,

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:45.880
um you're in the top 1% and you're going

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.040
to be and you're going to be a very

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.360
effective entrepreneur.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.360
Something you said along that and I

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.080
don't remember if it was in that

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.480
newsletter, but this is just a concept

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.200
I've been

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.880
continually coming back to. I would lead

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.600
our team meetings that on Tuesday

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.600
mornings with our team.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:08.400
Nobody wants to follow people who cannot

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.519
make decisions. Mhm. Mhm.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.640
And that concept of hey, if I don't know

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:14.880
the answer to this, I'm just going to

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.320
pick and make a decision one way or the

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.640
other. Mhm. And I'm going to be okay

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.920
with living with the consequences, but I

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.280
think what's important is making that

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.360
decision in that moment. Yeah.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.920
I have I have a lot of friends who are

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.440
high performers

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.239
at work. I mean I mean they earn two,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.040
three, 400,000 dollars a year working

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.959
for a company.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:39.640
And I love talking to them because

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:41.600
I I've I ask them all often like, "What

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.080
do What do you dislike about your job?"

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.080
And man, do [snorts] they give it to me.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.280
Like, they'll talk for half an hour

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.040
about what they dislike about their job.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.480
But, I like asking that question because

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.640
it helps me as a leader, as a business

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.120
owner, know what,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.800
you know, to watch out for.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.880
But, it almost all boils down to two

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.800
things.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.240
Number one, they know of a way to

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.920
improve their work,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.440
their customers' lives, their company,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.400
and they go to their boss and say, "Hey,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.440
like

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.920
I need this to change because it's hard

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.400
and it doesn't make any sense.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.600
I need you to make a decision here. Help

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.239
me."

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.200
And their boss doesn't make a decision.

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:19.640
Their boss will talk a big game. They'll

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.880
say, "Oh, yeah, that's so important.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.160
Like I'd love to help you." And then

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.280
absolutely nothing happens.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.000
And number two is they get surrounded by

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.320
They're surrounded by C players and low

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.640
performers and they're carrying the

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.120
weight of those low performers.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:36.360
So, as a boss, as a leader, I think it's

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.400
absolutely critical

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.720
that you make decisions.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.520
Obviously, making good decisions is

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.120
very, very important,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.360
but no decision is often worse than

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.480
making a bad decision.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.720
And then when it comes to firing your

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.880
low performers,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:53.360
man, it's it's really It's a crazy thing

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.120
because

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.280
the end of a like the end of a busy

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.960
period of my life, I was talking to a

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.320
mentor on the phone

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.200
and I was I was complaining about one of

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.760
my guys. I was like, "This guy is a pain

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.440
in the ass. He keeps making mistakes.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.440
Like I'm going to keep trying. I'm going

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.840
to keep training. I'm going to keep

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.040
trying to change them. It's on me as an

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.320
entrepreneur. Maybe I got to simplify

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.800
the job a little more. I got to do more

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:15.040
and more and more." He goes, "Nick, fire

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.480
him."

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.000
And I'm like, "Uh I can't. It's busy.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.960
Like I need him. That's more stress for

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.400
me." And he goes, "Nick,

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.320
here's something

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.000
true.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.000
The competence of your entire

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.520
organization falls to the level that you

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.440
tolerate.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:37.440
So, if you tolerate C players,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.840
your A players are going to walk away.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.760
And your entire organization will be a

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.840
C-level organization.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.360
So, if you want to

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.919
retain your A players, you have a

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.560
choice.

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:50.520
Like you can

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.680
put up with incompetence and watch them

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.840
walk away,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.320
or you can fire the poor performers

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.640
and make your A A players happy.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:01.320
So, it's a it's not always easy, but

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.800
letting people go and firing them is is

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.320
a very critical part of what we do as

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.320
entrepreneurs.

